{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"<!-- ROLE-MARKER: critic -->\n<!-- GENERIC bundle: minimal protocol sentences only (EXPERIMENTS.md B2).\n     Note: generic loom runs LOOM_MARATHON_CRITIC=off (the per-review LLM\n     critic is an [S] policy); this file exists for critic=auto variants. -->\n# Review Critic\n\nThe user message is a REVIEW CONTRACT (task, attempt, base, candidate,\ndesign). You are cwd-ed into a disposable checkout of the candidate commit.\nReview it against the task's design and acceptance criteria.\n\nWrite `CRITIC-VERDICT.txt` in the current directory; its FIRST line must be\nexactly one of, echoing the contract's values:\n\n    REVIEW attempt=<attempt> commit=<candidate> APPROVED — <reason>\n    REVIEW attempt=<attempt> commit=<candidate> CHANGES-REQUESTED — <reason>\n\nDo not modify the repository or any task state.\n\n\n\n### Multi-Agent Safety Rules\n\nYou are running in a parallel multi-agent environment. Follow these rules strictly:\n\n- **Only modify files directly related to your assigned task** — do not touch files outside your task scope\n- **Never run** `git stash`, `git checkout main`, or `git clean` outside your assigned worktree\n- **Never force-push or reset --hard** without explicit instruction from the user\n- **If you encounter files/changes from another agent**, leave them alone — do not modify, revert, or clean them up\n- **Commit only your changes** — do not stage unrelated modifications with `git add -A` or `git add .`; use specific file paths\n- **If your worktree has unexpected state**, report it via task notes or `loom complete` rather than cleaning it up\n- **Do not switch branches** — you are confined to your assigned worktree branch\n\n\n## User's Initial Request\n\nREVIEW CONTRACT\ntask=MARATHON-2\nattempt=1\nbase=adb99cc7cca7c31e6f61695da0471dc55fc3b6c8\ncandidate=e3282612631ec739f6e31b17e54d731898f7715f\n\nDESIGN:\n## Summary\n\nDeliver a bootable Huddle cluster skeleton at `/app`: a foreground `/app/start.sh` that launches local Redis on `127.0.0.1:6379` and three identical HTTP nodes on `127.0.0.1:8000/8001/8002`, plus `GET /api/health` returning `200 {\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":<0|1|2>}` with port→id mapping `8000→0`, `8001→1`, `8002→2`. This is the foundation every later API/realtime/SPA task mounts onto. No remote services and no Docker-in-Docker. Auth, workspaces, SPA, IRC, WebSocket fan-out, and durable schemas are out of scope here; this task only creates process supervision, shared config conventions, and a stub API router that subsequent tasks extend.\n\n## Technical Approach\n\n**Decision:** One Python 3.12 process image, three OS processes, FastAPI + Uvicorn, supervised by bash `start.sh`, with local `redis-server` as a fourth supervised child.\n\n**Why this shape**\n- The product requires three distinct HTTP listeners and node-kill resilience; three OS processes (not one multi-bind process, not threads-only) match the grader’s SIGKILL model and the 60s auto-restart requirement.\n- FastAPI gives a single mountable `APIRouter` tree under `/api` so MARATHON-3+ can add modules without rewriting the HTTP entrypoint.\n- Redis is started now even though health does not use it, because later fan-out and the Redis-outage tests assume the daemon is owned by `start.sh`.\n- Language/framework are unspecified by the product instruction; Python 3.12 is present in the harness and fits REST + later WebSocket work.\n\n**Alternatives considered**\n1. **Node.js `http` / Express** — viable (Node is installed), but the rest of the epic’s backend tasks are naturally one shared Python package with SQLite; splitting runtimes would complicate shared state.\n2. **Python stdlib only** — avoids deps, but forces every later task to reinvent routing, JSON validation, multipart, and WebSockets; rejected for velocity and consistency.\n3. **One process binding all three ports** — fails the “kill one HTTP node” resilience model; rejected.\n4. **Docker Compose / containers** — prohibited (no DinD / no remote orchestration).\n\n**Trade-off accepted:** `start.sh` must ensure FastAPI/Uvicorn (and `redis` client later) are available via a local venv + pinned `requirements.txt`, preferably with wheels under `vendor/` so boot does not depend on live PyPI. Cold start is slightly heavier than stdlib, but the router seam for sibling tasks is far cleaner.\n\n**Conventions this design locks for the epic (no sibling designs exist yet)**\n- Product name: **Huddle**; repo root is `/app` at runtime.\n- HTTP bind: `127.0.0.1` only; ports `8000/8001/8002`.\n- `node_id` ∈ `{0,1,2}` derived solely from port (never from startup order).\n- API prefix: `/api/...`; JSON only for API responses.\n- Primary durable store (for later tasks): SQLite file at `/app/data/huddle.db` shared by all nodes via the local filesystem. Redis is **not** the source of truth for users/messages — it is for pub/sub fan-out (and optional caches) in later tasks.\n- Env contract for each node process:\n  - `HUDDLE_NODE_ID` — integer `0|1|2`\n  - `HUDDLE_PORT` — integer `8000|8001|8002`\n  - `HUDDLE_HOST` — `127.0.0.1`\n  - `HUDDLE_REDIS_URL` — `redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0`\n  - `HUDDLE_DATA_DIR` — `/app/data` (or `$APP_ROOT/data`)\n- Error bodies for unspecified errors: FastAPI default `{\"detail\": ...}` until a later design overrides.\n- Health **must not** check Redis or SQLite liveness; a live HTTP process alone is enough for `200`.\n\n## Interface Contracts\n\n### Process / ops seam (verifier ↔ `start.sh`)\n\n| Concern | Contract |\n|---|---|\n| Entrypoint | Verifier runs `/app/start.sh` (executable). |\n| Lifetime | `start.sh` stays in the **foreground** until killed; while it is alive the cluster serves. |\n| Children | Exactly three HTTP node processes + one `redis-server` (plus any short-lived helpers). |\n| Ports | HTTP: `127.0.0.1:8000`, `:8001`, `:8002`. Redis: `127.0.0.1:6379`. |\n| Restart | If an HTTP child exits for any reason (including SIGKILL\n\nYou are cwd-ed into a DISPOSABLE detached checkout of the candidate commit. Review it against the design and acceptance criteria, then write CRITIC-VERDICT.txt in the current directory containing EXACTLY one first line:\nREVIEW attempt=1 commit=e3282612631ec739f6e31b17e54d731898f7715f APPROVED — <reason>\nor\nREVIEW attempt=1 commit=e3282612631ec739f6e31b17e54d731898f7715f CHANGES-REQUESTED — <reason>\n\nAddress this request using the lead mode conventions above."}]},"session_id":"16882a43-a73e-4b3c-8bdc-2189ba6eea40"}
